{
  "A document, a group or a floating window can move into its own browser window and back. Its content moves with it without being recreated, and the new window is part of the same manager, so its tabs, menus, keys and dragging work the same way.": "",
  "Moving to a new window and back": "",
  "Type in the form, choose a city and scroll the orders, then move the tab into a new window. The window opens beside the group at the same size, and shows the same component in the same state. Its title is that of the active document. When you close the window, its documents return to their original group.": "",
  "\"Move into new window\" moves the document or the whole group, or on a floating window's strip, the whole window. In a new window, \"Move back to main window\" returns the document or group.": "",
  "Ctrl + K, then Shift + O moves the active document into a new window (`moveToNewWindow`). Ctrl + K, then Shift + D moves it back (`moveToMainWindow`).": "",
  "Releasing outside the page opens a new window there with the dragged item (`dragOutToNewWindow`). Dragging out of a new window returns the item to the main window, or to the window under the pointer.": "",
  "Tab and Shift + Tab move through the groups of every window, and past a floating window, the same arrow key selects a new window.": "",
  "`moveToNewWindow(doc | group | floatingWindow, { rect, url })`, `moveToMainWindow(doc | group | window)`, and `move`, `split`, `dock` and `float` with a group in any window.": "",
  "Closing, blocked windows and events": "",
  "When a new window closes, its documents either return or close (`newWindowCloseAction`). Each one raises `onClosing` as usual in the main window, so the prompt for unsaved changes appears there. Browsers open windows for a page only in response to a click or a key press, and may still block them. In that case, `onError` reports it and a floating window opens instead (`newWindowFallback`). In this example, a switch simulates a browser that blocks the window.": "",
  "Saving the layout with its windows": "",
  "`saveLayout` writes each new window with its position on the screen, its layout and the original group of its documents. By default, `loadLayout` restores them as floating windows. To open them as windows again (`restoreWindows: 'reopen'`), the browser requires a click or a key press. If `loadLayout` is called from one, they open immediately. A page that loads its layout on startup shows them as floating windows, with a button that calls `reopenWindows()`.": "",
  "What works in a new window": "",
  "Everything a group does: pinning, previews, the \"…\" list, the recently used list, splitting, and resizing with the mouse and keyboard. Maximize is available only in the main window.": "",
  "They open in the new window's own top layer, because the kit's `Popup`, `ContextMenu` and `Tooltip` use the document of the element they open at.": "",
  "The manager's keys, control shortcuts and access keys all work, because the kit listens in every connected document (`connectDocument`).": "",
  "Any component, such as a form with a `DropDown`, a `DataGrid` with its filter popup and keys, or an `MxForm` dialog opened from it in that window.": "",
  "Inside the window, the same as in the page: strips, the zones or guides of a group, and the edges of its layout. Dock areas and floating are available only in the main window.": "",
  "The page's style sheets (including constructed ones) and the attributes of `<html>` and `<body>` (`data-theme`, `data-identity`, density, `dir`, `lang`, classes) are copied and kept in sync, so a theme change applies to every window.": "",
  "Reason": "",
  "An `<iframe>` in a document reloads when it moves to another window, as browsers do with every moved frame.": "",
  "The MotifJS core waits for the page's own document, so the manager lets content it creates in a new window attach to the page briefly first. A component that an application creates later inside such content does not receive `onMounted`.": "",
  "Browsers report when a window is resized, but not when it moves, so `rect` is read when it is needed.": "",
  "The window is already gone before anything can prevent it. Its documents return or close, each raising its event in the main window, and `onWindowClosing` cannot keep it open.": "",
  "Its content is a `main` landmark named after the manager. Its groups, strips and tabs work as they do in the page, with the same roving Tab stop.": "",
  "When the user moves a document to a new window, focus moves to it there. When it moves back, focus returns to it in the main window.": "",
  "Announced politely in the window that has focus: moved to a new window, returned to the main window, window closed, and browser did not open the window.": "",
  "Allows documents, groups and floating windows to move into new windows. A document's own `allowNewWindow` takes precedence.": "",
  "The page a new window opens, on the page's own origin (an empty page of the application). The move waits for it to load.": "",
  "The window's title. `{title}` is the title of the active document and `{page}` is the page title, and it stays up to date.": "",
  "The initial size of a new window. `null` uses the size of what moves into it.": "",
  "What happens when the browser does not open a window: a floating window in the page, or nothing.": "",
  "Whether a new window's documents return to the main window (into their original group) or close when it closes. `onWindowClosing` can change this each time.": "",
  "How `loadLayout` restores saved new windows: as floating windows, as windows again (see below), or as groups at the right of the main window.": "",
  "Opens the window, for an application shell, a host page with its own frame, or a test. `request` is `{ url, name, features, rect }`.": "",
  "Dragging past the edge of the browser window opens a new window there, and dragging past the edge of a new window returns the item.": "",
  "`target` is a document, a group or a floating window, and `options` is `{ rect, url }` (`rect` on the screen). Returns `null` when the move is not allowed, `onWindowOpening` cancels it, or the browser opens no window (which also raises `onError`).": "",
  "Returns a document, a group or a `TabBrowserWindow` to its original group, otherwise to the active group of the main window. A window left empty closes.": "",
  "Also applies to a new window, where closing follows `newWindowCloseAction`.": "",
  "Reopens saved windows that the browser blocked (`restoreWindows: 'reopen'`). Call it from a click or a key press.": "",
  "The new windows, with the most recently used last.": "",
  "Also includes `browserWindows`: `rect` on the screen, the layout, and the original group (`home`). Versions 1 and 2 still load.": "",
  "TabBrowserWindow": "",
  "The browser window.": "",
  "The manager's part in that window (`.mx-tabdocs.mx-tabdocs__page`), which fills it.": "",
  "Its position on the screen and the size of its page, in pixels.": "",
  "Its contents. The title follows `activeDocument`.": "",
  "The new window containing it, or `null` in the main window (also on `TabGroup`).": "",
  "`{ document, group, window, rect, url }`, cancelable, and `rect` and `url` can be changed (`window` is the floating window being moved). Then `{ window, document, group }`.": "",
  "Also applies to new windows, with `kind: 'floating' | 'browser'` and `then: 'back' | 'close'` (which can be changed). `reason` then is `'closed'`, `'docked'` (returned), `'empty'` or `'moved'` (a floating window that moved into a new window).": "",
  "`{ error, document, group, fallback }`, where `error` is `'windowBlocked'` (no window opened) or `'windowNotUsable'` (a page from another origin). `fallback` can be changed.": "",
  "A document in a closed window closes in the main window (`newWindowCloseAction: 'close'`), so the prompt for a dirty document appears there.": "",
  "Also includes `'newWindow'` and `'mainWindow'`, with `window`.": "",
  "Additional `target` types: `'new-window'` (with `rect` on the screen) and `'main-window'`. An `'edge'` position in a new window has `browserWindow`.": "",
  "For other windows in the kit": "",
  "The kit's document-level listeners (keys, access keys, tooltips, the focus guard) also work in another same-origin document, and are disconnected when its window closes.": "",
  "Copies the page's style sheets and the attributes of `<html>` and `<body>` into another document and keeps them in sync. Returns a function that stops this.": "",
  "A `ResizeObserver` that follows its elements into another window, because a browser's observer only reports elements of its own window.": "",
  "See the [overview](/tabdocs#api), [Tabs](/tabdocs/tabs), [Menus and keys](/tabdocs/menus), [Drag and drop](/tabdocs/drag) and [Dock and floating windows](/tabdocs/dock) for the rest of the API.": ""
}
